A||N||8||bHooper||operating temps for a '86 GT (Help!)||||2||
Z||000000||bHooper||08-04-2000||09:32 AM||||Hey all... i finally got to put some serious mileage on my GT for the first time sense I put it back together in May.  On tuesday, I took 2 seperate trips, one about 90 miles round trip and the other about 120.<P>On the second trip, I was cruising about 75 mph and the car temp had been showing 220 degrees.  It got a little over that and the temp light came on.  So I pulled off the highway, turned the car off, then back to start (so the fan could run) and the temp was almost in the <B>RED</B>!<P>I check the thermostat (boiling water test) and it opened fine.  I have not flushed the radiator, but it does have plenty of new coolent.<P>Any thoughts?  <P>p.s.<BR>I also got code 32, 3 times on that same trip.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||2
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||08-04-2000||09:52 AM||||Code 32 indicates an EGR failure. Could be a false code set by engine temp. Check your fan circut operation (ADAL connector short A & B fan should come on Eng./motor not running) If it doesn't come on trouble shoot the circut. Also check the EGR operation!<BR>Also turn the A/C on and the fan should come on as this is normal in the A/C mode if is has a charge. It it over heats while driving  and the A/C does operate the fan turn it on and leave car running (if your water pump is working) and your temp should come down.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||08-04-2000||10:03 AM||||i currently have the a/c lines to the compresor disconected.  will it cause any problems to select the A/C without the lines hooked up(there isnt any freon in the system)?<P>with the vent"on" the fan and the blower (v6) both run.<P>whould these run when i turn the car off (like my girlfriends accord)?  it does not appear to do this.<P>i thought about removeing the thermostat (just to be sure) and driving it home without that this afternoon.<P>also, Rodney Dickman has a fan switch that comes on at 220 degrees and off at 180, with a 180 degree thermostat.  is that something that i should investigate useing?<P>thanks<BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||08-04-2000||02:50 PM||||at lunch, the temp hung around 220.  although i didnt really do anything dramatic.<P>i did notice a new problem with the voltage gage... at stop lights the voltage would get weak and the light would come on!  the car has a new altenator/battery and cables!  although i have not put the heat shield on the altenator, yet... i plan to do that this weekend.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||08-06-2000||08:48 PM||||ok... i got to work on the car today and this is what i did.  i reinstalled the heat shield that goes over the crossover to defelct heat from the egr valve and the one beside the valve.  i still have 2 shields left (one looks like a shark fin) the other is hard to describe.<P>also, i reinstalled the air deflecter that goes underneath the car (best guess is that this deflects air into the radiator).  and i reinstalled the heat shield for the altenator.<P>i did not get a chance to test voltage on the fan. but it does run when i turn the ac (no freon) on (and the temp gauge goes down).<P>i did take the car for a spin around town, but this hasnt been my main problem... i will see how it acts on the highway tommorow (this has been the problem) <P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||08-06-2000||09:49 PM||||you need to check the fan switch at the engine and the circut for it. To test circut simply locate sensor, disconnect it, jumper the plug conectors to each other and turn on the key. The fan should come on if circut is good. As I recall the stock switches for fan are supposed to come on some where between 115 & 125 degrees. If your fan doesn't come on when you jumper the plug terminals together more trouble shooting will be necessary!Let me know.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||08-07-2000||01:27 AM||||The stock fan switch turns on at 232 degrees.  If the trunk fan turns on but the radiator fan doesn't when you ground the circuit, check the fan relay.  It's right in front of the driver's side headlight.  Nevermind just saw that your fan does work..<P>Are you sure your radiator isn't all clogged up or something?  No restrictions in the cooling system?  Seems like it is running awful hot..<P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 148,000 miles and counting!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||08-07-2000||08:43 AM||||ok, here's my initial observation (after driving to work).<P>the car weemed to run much cooler.  it did heat up to around 200-210 degrees at a stop light, but once i got back on the parkway... it cooled right back down!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>conclusion... the flexible spoiler (underneath the car) does deflect air upwards to the radiator.  is this the total solution?... probably not, but it did appear better (at the moment).<P>i will be driving to Winston Salem, this morning, that will be the big test.  it is a 40 mile (round trip) drive, all on the highway.<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||08-07-2000||12:54 PM||||i didnt take that trip, as mentioned earlier, but i did take the car out to lunch... and in the 90+ degree weather with traffic and stoplights... <B>the car never got above 180!!</B><P>needless to say i am hapy!  does that mean that the entire problem is fixed... no.  but it does mean that it is not as big a problem as previously anticipated.<P>i will check the circuitry later this weak.<P>lesson learned... reinstall all heat shields and air deflectors, prior to driving!<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
